[[Shawn Levy]] became the studio's top choice to direct ''Avengers 5'' by mid-March 2024, after he directed the MCU film ''[[Deadpool & Wolverine]]'' (2024), but there were potential scheduling issues with his other projects.<ref name="LevyEyedTHR" /><ref name="LevyEyed" /> By that May, Marvel Studios had approached [[Robert Downey Jr.]]—who starred as [[Tony Stark (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Tony Stark / Iron Man]] in "[[The Infinity Saga]]", covering the MCU's [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase One|Phases One]], [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase Two|Two]], and [[Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase Three|Three]]—about returning to the MCU to take on the new character of [[Victor von Doom (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Victor von Doom / Doctor Doom]]. Downey said he would only return if the Russo brothers also returned as directors.<ref name="DowneyRussosPaySneider" /><ref name="DowneyRussosPayVariety" /><ref name="DowneyTHRAug2024" /> After McFeely approached the Russos with an idea for the films that excited them,<ref name="RussosPitchMar2025" /> they entered early talks to direct both ''Avengers 5'' and ''Secret Wars'' by mid-July.<ref name="RussosEarlyTalks" /> During SDCC at the end of the month, the Russos were confirmed to be directing the two films.<ref name="SDCC2024Avengers2" /> They revealed that ''Avengers 5'' was now titled ''Avengers: Doomsday'', and both films would feature Downey as Doctor Doom.<ref name="SDCC2024Avengers" /><!-- The Russos were producing the films through their production company [[AGBO]], and --> McFeely had joined as writer for both films,<ref name="SDCC2024Avengers2" /><ref name="RussosAvengersProduce" /> working with Waldron.<ref name="FeigeConferenceJul2025THR" /> [[Chris McKenna (writer)|Chris McKenna]] and [[Erik Sommers]], writers on the [[Spider-Man (2017 film series)|MCU ''Spider-Man'' films]], also worked on the scripts for ''Doomsday'' and ''Secret Wars'',<ref name="McKennaSommers" /> though McKenna said their work was mainly focused on writing ''Secret Wars'', providing "a little bit" of help on ''Doomsday''.<ref name="McKennaSWWork" /><ref name="AvengersDDFinalWriters" /> McFeely and Waldron received final screenwriting credits for ''Doomsday''.<ref name="AvengersDDFinalWriters" /> Marvel Studios co-president Louis D'Esposito and executive Jonathan Schwartz also produce ''Doomsday'' alongside Feige.<ref name="DoomsdayProducers" /> On casting Downey as Doom, Feige said, "He played the most iconic hero. Let's have him play the most iconic villain."<ref name="FeigeRussosCinemaConDoomEW" /> Feige stated that "[[Time Runs Out]]", the prelude storyline for the 2015–16 "Secret Wars", also served as inspiration for the two films,<ref name="FeigeTimeRunsOut" /><ref name="TimeRunsOutCollider" /> and said one of their goals was to give closure to Marvel storylines that predated the MCU, including those of characters from [[20th Century Fox]]'s [[X-Men (film series)|''X-Men'' film series]].<ref name="FeigeShanghaiMediaJul2026" />

In March 2025, Marvel announced 26 cast members for the film alongside Downey. Many of those actors returned from previous MCU media and some were reprising their roles from Fox's ''X-Men'' films.<ref name="CastAnnouncementTHR" /><ref name="CastAnnouncementMarvel" /> Downey, Marvel Studios, and Feige teased that there were more cast members to reveal,<ref name="CastAnnouncementLivestreamViews" /><ref name="FeigeCinemaConSR" /> including the return of [[Chris Evans (actor)|Chris Evans]] as [[Steve Rogers (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Steve Rogers]];<ref name="RogersTeaserOnlineTotalFilm" /> the Russos felt the correct way for them to return to the MCU would be to continue his story, and Joe Russo said the story they came up with for the films had integral roles for both Downey and Evans.<ref name="RussosFandangoApr2026" /> By September 2026, some additional cast members had been revealed, with the full cast still yet to be announced.<ref name="DoomsdayCastTHRSep2026" /> ''Doomsday'' features groups of heroes from three different universes—the [[Avengers (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Avengers]], [[Wakanda (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wakandans]], and [[New Avengers (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|New Avengers]] from [[Earth-616 (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Earth-616]]; the [[Fantastic Four (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Fantastic Four]] from [[Earth-828]]; and the "original" [[X-Men (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|X-Men]] from [[Earth-10005]]—converging to face Doom.<ref name="FeigeRussosCinemaConEW" /><ref name="FeigeCinemaConSR" /><ref name="EncoreScenesEW" /> Feige said they were using the ''Secret Wars'' idea of different universes colliding to unite cast members from different film franchises, and that ''Doomsday'' was picking up where ''Endgame'' left off.<ref name="FeigeRussosCinemaConEW" />

Production designer Gavin Bocquet and cinematographer [[Newton Thomas Sigel]] reteamed with the Russo brothers after working on their television series ''[[Citadel (TV series)|Citadel]]'' (2023–present); Sigel also worked on the Russos' film ''[[Cherry (2021 film)|Cherry]]'' (2021) and a selection of Fox's ''X-Men'' films.<ref name="Bahrain" /><ref name="Bocquet" /> Costume designer [[Judianna Makovsky]] returned from previous MCU films.<ref name="Makovsky" /> The Russos said ''Doomsday'' and ''Secret Wars'' were being produced together in a similar way to ''Infinity War'' and ''Endgame'', but they would not be filmed [[Back-to-back film production|back-to-back]] like those films were.<ref name="NoBackToBack" /> [[Principal photography]] for ''Doomsday'' began on April 28, 2025,<ref name="FilmingDayOne" /> at [[Pinewood Studios]] in Buckinghamshire, England,<ref name="PinewoodFilming" /> under the [[working title]] ''Apple Pie 1''.<ref name="WorkingTitle" /> In May 2025, Disney delayed the release date to December 18, 2026, to allow more time for production to be completed.<ref name="May2025ReleaseDelay" /> Location filming was taking place in [[Bahrain]] by the end of the month,<ref name="Bahrain" /> and was also set to occur at [[Windsor Great Park]] in June and August.<ref name="WindsorFilming" /><ref name="WindsorFilming2" /><ref name="WindsorFilming3" /> Filming also took place at [[Shinfield Studios]] in [[Berkshire]], England.<ref name="ShinfieldFilming" /> Filming [[Wrap (filmmaking)|wrapped]] on September 19, 2025.<ref name="FilmingWrap" /> [[Jeffrey Ford (film editor)|Jeffrey Ford]] and [[Matthew Schmidt]] are editing the film, having worked on previous MCU media.<ref name="Ford" />{{third-party inline|date=March 2025}}<ref name="Schmidt" />{{third-party inline|date=August 2026}} ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' reported that there would be a "healthy" amount of additional photography,<ref name="FilmingWrap" /> which took place in early-to-mid-2026.<ref name="AdditionalFilming" /><ref name="HarbourFeb2026" /><ref name="HarbourJun2026" />

== Music ==
In April 2024, [[Alan Silvestri]] indicated that he could be composing the music for an upcoming MCU project after previously doing so for ''Infinity War'', ''Endgame'', and other MCU films.<ref name="SilvestriApr2024" /> That July, he was confirmed to be returning as the composer for both ''Doomsday'' and ''Secret Wars''.<ref name="SilvestriJul2024" />

== Marketing ==
{{Main|Marketing of Avengers: Doomsday{{!}}Marketing of ''Avengers: Doomsday''}}
Following the announcement of Downey's casting as Doom and other details at SDCC in July 2024,<ref name="SDCC2024Avengers" /> several unique marketing stunts were used for ''Doomsday''. Many cast members were announced as part of a nearly five-and-a-half-hour-long [[livestream]] in March 2025, which became the most-viewed cross-platform livestream stunt.<ref name="CastAnnouncementTHR" /><ref name="CastAnnouncementLivestreamViews" /> Four short teasers premiered in theaters ahead of showings for the film ''[[Avatar: Fire and Ash]]'' (2025), including one that revealed Evans's return as Rogers.<ref name="FirstTeaserRogersVariety" /><ref name="FourDifferentTrailers" /> These teasers were leaked upon their debuts, which Marvel had expected, and were officially released online soon after.<ref name="FourTrailersViews" /> The first full trailer debuted at [[CinemaCon]] in April 2026,<ref name="CinemaCon2026Variety" /> and was met with mixed responses when it was released online in July. A "special look" focusing on Doom received more positive responses when it was released in August, after being shown at SDCC 2026 and Disney's [[D23 (Disney)#2026|D23]] convention.<ref name="D232026Takeaways" /> A common element of the teasers and trailers is a clock counting down to the film's release.<ref name="USATodayCountdownClock" /> The final trailer was released in November 2026.<ref name="EmpireFinalTrailer" />

A re-release of ''Endgame'', subtitled ''[[Avengers Endgame: Encore|Encore]]'', was released in September 2026,<ref name="EncoreTicketPresale" /> with new footage that acts as a narrative bridge between ''Endgame'' and ''Doomsday'', enhancing the story of the latter.<ref name="EncoreNewFootage" /><ref name="EncoreRussosTHR" /> The additional footage was shot during production of ''Doomsday''.<ref name="EncoreEmpireRussos" /> The ''Countdown to Avengers: Doomsday'' official podcast, hosted by [[Iman Vellani]]—who stars as [[Kamala Khan (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Kamala Khan / Ms. Marvel]] in the MCU—and podcaster Frank Alvarez, premiered on August 27, 2026. The five-episode weekly podcast recaps key MCU moments leading up to ''Doomsday''.<ref name="OfficialPodcast" /><ref name="50MillionPreSales" /><ref name="EncoreTicketPresale" /> Six additional podcast episodes began releasing weekly on November 5, 2026, covering the original Fox ''X-Men'' trilogy and several films of the Multiverse Saga.<ref name="MoreOfficialPodcastEps" />{{third party inline|date=November 2026}}<ref name="MoreOfficialPodcast" /> The film also had numerous brand partnerships,<ref name="PartnershipsAttractionsMag" /> along with merchandise and clothing items.<ref name="MarvelMustHaves" /> The global promotional tour began in Las Vegas on November 21, 2026, at the [[Formula One]] [[2026 Las Vegas Grand Prix]], and will continue in São Paulo, the Gold Coast in Queensland, Rome, London, Seoul, Shanghai, Toronto, and Los Angeles.<ref name="LasVegasPromotion2" />

== Release ==
''Avengers: Doomsday'' will have its world premiere on December 14, 2026, in Los Angeles,<ref name="NoAwardsScreenings" /> at the [[Intuit Dome]],<ref name="WorldPremiere" />{{better source needed|date=November 2026}} and is scheduled to be released in the United Kingdom on December 16,<ref name="UKRelease" /> and in the United States in over 4,000 theaters on December 18.<ref name="PFLTicketSales" /><ref name="May2025ReleaseDelay" /> It was originally scheduled for May 2, 2025,<ref name="SDCC2022" /> but was delayed to May 1, 2026, in part due to the 2023 Writers Guild of America strike,<ref name="2026DelayReason" /><ref name="SDCC2024Avengers" /> followed by another delay to the December 2026 date to allow more time for production to be completed.<ref name="May2025ReleaseDelay" /> It will be part of Phase Six of the MCU.<ref name="SDCC2022" /><ref name="DigitalSpyPhases" />

In early 2026, ''Doomsday'' was revealed to not have access to [[IMAX]] theaters in the United States for at least three weeks because the film ''[[Dune: Part Three]]'', which is also scheduled for release on December 18, has exclusivity in that time frame.<ref name="VarietyDoomsdayDuneMar2026" /><ref name="IMAXRelease" /> ''Doomsday'' is expected to be released in IMAX in select international markets,<ref name="IMAXRelease" /> and will also be available in other [[premium large format]]s (PLFs) such as [[Dolby Vision]], [[ScreenX]],<ref name="VarietyDoomsdayDuneMar2026" /> [[D-Box]], and [[4DX]],<ref name="VarietyDoomsdayDuneMar2026" /><ref name="WrapDisneyPLFPush" /> as well as [[RealD 3D]].<ref name="PLFPosters" /> ''[[TheWrap]]'' reported in March that Disney was planning to push hard to gain access to those other formats and to get into as many of the 322 chain-specific theater formats around the world as possible.<ref name="WrapDisneyPLFPush" /> The next month, Disney announced that ''Doomsday'' would receive the company's [[Infinity Vision]] certification for PLFs, which is intended to inform audiences which theaters offer "the biggest, brightest, and most immersive cinematic experiences" and meet certain technical standards.<ref name="InfinityVisionRelease" /> Many commentators felt Infinity Vision was created in response to ''Doomsday'' not being able to open in IMAX in the United States.<ref name="CinemaCon2026THR" /><ref name="InfinityVisionResponse" /> The Russos worked with ScreenX to create a specific version of the film for that format featuring expanded images.<ref name="ScreenXSpecific" /> In November 2026, ''[[Omelete]]'' reported that Infinity Vision–versions of the film would have approximately one additional minute of footage from regular versions.<ref name="OmeleteRuntimes" />

== Reception ==
=== Box office projections ===
Rebecca Rubin at ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' reported in March 2026 that early box office tracking was "strong" and there were predictions that ''Doomsday'' would be 2026's highest-grossing film.<ref name="VarietyDoomsdayDuneMar2026" /> Rubin and her colleague Jordan Moreau elaborated that Marvel Studios' recent box office track record had been "rocky", but the studio had success with the Avengers-style crossover films ''[[Spider-Man: No Way Home]]'' (2021) and ''Deadpool & Wolverine''. Theatrical exhibitors were excited for ''Doomsday'' considering ''Endgame''{{'s}} record-breaking run, and there was potential for the combined release of ''Doomsday'' and ''Dune: Part Three'' to result in one of the biggest box office weekends ever.<ref name="CinemaCon2026Variety" />

The film generated $16.5&nbsp;million in advance premium format ticket sales in the first 24 hours of sales in July 2026, more than double the pre-sales ''Deadpool & Wolverine'' had. Rubin said this was notable because the sales did not include IMAX. Many of the roughly 1,000 PLF theaters had sold out screenings on December 17 and 18, with theaters looking to add more screenings on Christmas Day and beyond.<ref name="PFLTicketSales" /> By the end of August, the film had grossed $35&nbsp;million in advance ticket sales, with Global Box Office reporting that it could earn $260–300&nbsp;million in its opening weekend.<ref name="AdvanceTicketsBOWknd1Aug2026" /> Pre-sales had reached $50&nbsp;million by early September 2026, 100 days before its release, becoming the first film to achieve this in that time frame.<ref name="50MillionPreSalesTF" /> Disney said 70 percent of advance tickets sold were for Infinity Vision showings.<ref name="50MillionPreSales" /> By the end of October, the film had reached $125&nbsp;million in pre-sales. Caroline Reid at ''[[Forbes]]'' said the strong pre-sales were being driven by "die-hard fans" who wanted to see the film on opening weekend, and questioned whether this would translate into continued box office success in subsequent weekends with general audiences who may be concerned about the amount of MCU projects required to understand ''Doomsday''{{'s}} story. She said the critical response would have an impact on the film's longevity at the box office.<ref name="125MillionPreSales" /> A month before the film's release, Disney announced there was a "spike" in ticket sales following the release of the ''[[VisionQuest]]'' (2026) finale, which included a [[Mid-credits and post-credits scenes in the Marvel Cinematic Universe|mid-credits scene]] featuring Doom and [[Wanda Maximoff (Marvel Cinematic Universe)|Wanda Maximoff / Scarlet Witch]] that was believed to be a tease for ''Doomsday''.<ref name="VQTicketSpike" /><ref name="VQPostCreditsDeadline" /><ref name="MatalasNov2026GD" /> Also at that time, Box Office Theory reported the film could earn $340&nbsp;million in its opening weekend.<ref name="BOTBOWnkd1Nov2026" />

=== Awards campaign ===
Clayton Davis of ''Variety'' revealed in November 2026 that the film would not be screened for awards contention before its world premiere, to prevent spoilers from being shared. Davis reported that the film would therefore not be under consideration for the [[Critics Choice Awards]], the [[Golden Globes]], the [[New York Film Critics Circle]], the [[National Board of Review]], the [[Los Angeles Film Critics Association]], the [[American Film Institute Awards]], the [[Artios Awards]], or the [[BAFTA Awards]]' longlists. Required material was submitted to the [[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] for consideration in some [[Academy Awards]] shortlists. Davis noted that the same approach was taken for the films ''[[Star Wars: The Force Awakens]]'' (2015) and ''[[Spider-Man: No Way Home]]'' (2021), which both received Academy Award nominations, and ''Doomsday'' would be released in time for consideration at the [[Actor Awards]].<ref name="NoAwardsScreenings" />

== Sequel ==
{{Further|Marvel Cinematic Universe: Phase Six#Avengers: Secret Wars (2027)}}
A sequel, ''Avengers: Secret Wars'', is scheduled to be released on December 17, 2027.<ref name="May2025ReleaseDelay" /> It is also being written by Waldron, McFeely, McKenna, and Sommers,<ref name="McKennaSommers" /> and directed by the Russo brothers.<ref name="SDCC2024Avengers" /> The film will conclude Phase Six and the Multiverse Saga of the MCU.<ref name="SDCC2022" /><ref name="DigitalSpyPhases" />

